### Download architecture

Implement a server-side download provider interface.

A provider must report its actual capabilities and current status.

If a provider is not configured, show an informative unavailable state.

Do not invent quality options or file sizes.

Do not show a fake progress bar.

If actual byte-level progress is available, report it.

Otherwise, show an indeterminate processing state.

Support cancellation only when the underlying task can actually be canceled.

Use server-side job identifiers for long-running jobs where necessary.

Prevent users from submitting arbitrary URLs that could access internal network resources or cloud metadata endpoints.

Implement SSRF protections, host allowlists, redirect validation, response size limits, timeouts, and safe content handling.

Never expose direct access to arbitrary backend URLs.

### Download history

Authenticated users can view their own history.

Allow deletion of history records after confirmation.

Only remove stored files when the user has the appropriate permission and the file is actually managed by the application.

