# PHASE 12 — REAL ANIME METADATA

## 12. Metadata provider

Use AniList or another suitable documented anime metadata provider.

Before integration, verify the provider's current API documentation, authentication requirements, rate limits, permitted use, and image-use terms.

Do not assume that a provider offers every field or feature.

Build an adapter layer rather than coupling components directly to provider-specific response formats.

The application should normalize external metadata into internal types.

Required normalized fields may include:

- ID.
- Title.
- Alternative titles.
- Synopsis.
- Genres.
- Cover image.
- Banner image.
- Release year.
- Release season.
- Status.
- Episode count.
- Characters when available.
- Related titles when available.

Only populate fields actually returned by the source.

Cache metadata where permitted.

Handle rate limits, network failures, unavailable records, and missing artwork.

Do not send an API key to the browser if the provider requires it to remain private.

Use server-side API calls where required.

If the provider permits public client-side requests without a secret, use the documented method appropriately.

Never scrape undocumented endpoints as a shortcut.

### Data synchronization

Distinguish between:

- Externally sourced metadata.
- Locally curated editorial content.
- Administrator-entered information.
- Actual release data.

Record metadata source information where useful.

Do not claim that an API automatically supplies verified daily episode releases unless the provider actually supports the required data.

Use scheduled synchronization only after the required backend and scheduler are configured.

Do not invent release times.

